---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import BitChip from "../../components/BitChip.astro";
import { LEARN_TOPICS, getAdjacentLearnTopics } from "../../lib/learnTopics";

const CURRENT_HREF = "/learn/ipv4-vs-ipv6";
const currentTopic = LEARN_TOPICS.find((topic) => topic.href === CURRENT_HREF)!;
const { prev, next } = getAdjacentLearnTopics(CURRENT_HREF);

const ipv4Octets = "192.168.1.1".split(".").map((octet) => ({
	decimal: octet,
	bits: Number(octet).toString(2).padStart(8, "0"),
}));

const comparisonRows = [
	{ standard: "IPv4", bits: "32", addresses: "~4.3 billion" },
	{ standard: "IPv6", bits: "128", addresses: "~340 undecillion (340 followed by 36 zeros)" },
];

const fullIpv6Example = "2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334";
const hexSegment = "8a2e";
const hexNibbles = hexSegment.split("").map((char) => ({
	hex: char.toUpperCase(),
	bits: parseInt(char, 16).toString(2).padStart(4, "0"),
}));
---

<Layout
	title="IPv4 vs IPv6"
	description="Why the internet needed a bigger address space: IPv4's 32-bit limit, IPv6's 128-bit fix, and what scalability means in computing."
	ogType="article"
>
	<p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/learn">Learn</a> / {currentTopic.title}</p>

	<section class="hero">
		<h1>IPv4 vs IPv6</h1>
		<p class="hero-lead">
			Every device on the internet needs an address. Here's why we had to invent a second kind.
		</p>
	</section>

	<div class="blocks">
		<section class="block">
			<p class="block-title">What Is an IP Address?</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				Every device connected to the internet — your phone, your laptop, a server halfway across
				the world — needs a unique address, the same way every house needs a unique street address
				for mail to find it. This is called an IP address (Internet Protocol address). Underneath,
				like everything else in computing, it's just binary.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="block block--example">
			<p class="block-title">IPv4: The Original Design</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				When the modern internet's addressing system was designed in the early 1980s, each address
				was made of 32 bits. You've already seen what that means: 32 bits gives you 2 to the power
				of 32 possible values.
			</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				That's <span class="mono-highlight">4,294,967,296</span> — about 4.3 billion possible
				addresses.
			</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				An IPv4 address is usually written as four numbers separated by dots — like{" "}
				<span class="mono-highlight">192.168.1.1</span>. Each of those numbers is actually one byte
				(8 bits), so the whole address is really just 32 bits in a more readable form. That's also
				why each number is always between 0 and 255 — the exact range a single byte can hold, as you
				saw back in <a href="/learn/reading-a-byte">Reading a Byte</a>.
			</p>

			<div class="octet-demo">
				{
					ipv4Octets.map((octet) => (
						<div class="octet-demo-group">
							<span class="octet-demo-label">{octet.decimal}</span>
							<div class="octet-demo-tiles">
								{octet.bits.split("").map((bit) => (
									<BitChip value={bit as "0" | "1"} />
								))}
							</div>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</div>

			<p class="block-text">
				In 1981, 4.3 billion felt limitless. Computers were rare and expensive, confined mostly to
				universities, governments, and large companies. Nobody was imagining a future where a
				lightbulb, a doorbell, or a wristwatch might need its own address.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<p class="block-title">The Problem: Running Out of Room</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				Fast forward a few decades. Personal computers, then smartphones, then smart TVs, doorbells,
				fitness trackers, and countless other devices all needed their own address to join the
				internet. 4.3 billion addresses — once unthinkably large — started running out.
			</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				This is the same doubling principle you've already seen: every extra bit doubles how many
				values you can represent. 32 bits sounded huge, but it's still a fixed number. Once the
				number of devices in the world caught up to it, there was nowhere left to grow.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="block block--example">
			<p class="block-title">IPv6: Solving It With Scale</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				The fix wasn't to add a few more bits — it was to add a lot more. IPv6 addresses use 128
				bits instead of 32.
			</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				Because each extra bit doubles the address space, jumping from 32 bits to 128 bits doesn't
				just make the space bigger — it makes it almost incomprehensibly larger:
			</p>

			<div class="compare-table-wrap">
				<table class="compare-table">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Standard</th>
							<th>Bits</th>
							<th>Possible Addresses</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						{
							comparisonRows.map((row) => (
								<tr>
									<td class="compare-standard">{row.standard}</td>
									<td class="compare-bits">{row.bits}</td>
									<td class="compare-addresses">{row.addresses}</td>
								</tr>
							))
						}
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			<div class="scale-bar">
				<div class="scale-row">
					<span class="scale-label">IPv4</span>
					<div class="scale-track">
						<div class="scale-fill scale-fill--v4" style="width: 3%" />
					</div>
					<span class="scale-value">~4.3 billion</span>
				</div>
				<div class="scale-row">
					<span class="scale-label">IPv6</span>
					<div class="scale-track">
						<div class="scale-fill scale-fill--v6" style="width: 100%" />
					</div>
					<span class="scale-value">~340 undecillion</span>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p class="scale-caption">Illustrative, not to scale — IPv4's true share would be far too small to see.</p>

			<p class="block-text">
				To put 340 undecillion in perspective: it's roughly enough to assign a unique address to
				every grain of sand on Earth — many times over — with room to spare.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="block block--example">
			<p class="block-title">Written Differently, Too</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				128 bits is a lot to write out in binary — 128 digits long. So IPv6 addresses are written in
				hexadecimal instead, the same shorthand you met on the{" "}
				<a href="/learn/binary-hex-decimal">Binary ↔ Hex ↔ Decimal</a> page. A real IPv6 address looks
				like this:
			</p>

			<p class="ipv6-address-display">{fullIpv6Example}</p>

			<p class="block-text">
				Take just one group from that address, <span class="mono-highlight">{hexSegment}</span> —
				each of its hex digits is still just a 4-bit binary nibble underneath:
			</p>

			<div class="hexbit-demo">
				{
					hexNibbles.map((nibble) => (
						<div class="hexbit-demo-group">
							<span class="hexbit-demo-label">{nibble.hex}</span>
							<div class="hexbit-demo-tiles">
								{nibble.bits.split("").map((bit) => (
									<BitChip value={bit as "0" | "1"} />
								))}
							</div>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</div>

			<p class="block-text">
				Each group of four hex digits represents 16 bits — exactly the kind of hex-to-binary
				relationship you've already seen, just applied at a much larger scale.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<p class="block-title">Why This Matters: Designing for Growth</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				This is a good example of a much bigger idea in computing: <em>scalability</em> — designing
				a system so it can keep working well even as it grows far beyond what its creators
				originally expected.
			</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				The early internet's designers weren't wrong to use 32 bits — it was more than enough for
				what existed at the time. But a truly scalable system has to plan for growth it can't fully
				predict. IPv6 exists because the internet's open, standardized design let new devices keep
				joining for decades — and eventually, that same openness meant it needed a fundamentally
				bigger address space to keep up.
			</p>
			<p class="block-text">
				This same idea — building room to grow before you desperately need it — shows up everywhere
				in computing: databases, network infrastructure, even the way software is architected from
				day one.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="block">
			<div class="learn-pagination">
				{
					prev && (
						<a class="learn-pagination-link learn-pagination-prev" href={prev.href}>
							← {prev.title}
						</a>
					)
				}
				{
					next &&
						(next.live ? (
							<a class="learn-pagination-link learn-pagination-next" href={next.href}>
								{next.title} →
							</a>
						) : (
							<span class="learn-pagination-link learn-pagination-next learn-pagination-soon">
								{next.title} (coming soon)
							</span>
						))
				}
			</div>
		</section>
	</div>
</Layout>

<style>
	.breadcrumb {
		margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
		text-align: center;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.breadcrumb a {
		color: #ffae2e;
		font-weight: 600;
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	.breadcrumb a:hover {
		text-decoration: underline;
	}

	.hero {
		text-align: center;
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
	}

	.hero h1 {
		margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
		font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 5vw, 2.1rem);
	}

	.hero-lead {
		margin: 0 auto;
		max-width: 34rem;
		font-size: 1rem;
	}

	.blocks {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.75rem;
	}

	.block {
		background: var(--color-surface);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
		border-radius: 14px;
		padding: 1rem 1.15rem;
	}

	.block-title {
		margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.1em;
		color: #ffae2e;
	}

	.block-text {
		margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
		font-size: 1rem;
	}

	.block-text:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.block-text em {
		color: var(--color-text);
		font-style: normal;
		font-weight: 700;
	}

	.mono-highlight {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	.block--example {
		text-align: center;
	}

	/* --- IPv4 octet visual: same "grouped tiles + label" language as the
	   byte-demo/nibble-demo patterns on other Learn pages, but with the
	   label above the tiles instead of below — reads more like an
	   annotation pointing down at the bits it decodes to. */
	.octet-demo {
		display: flex;
		align-items: flex-start;
		justify-content: center;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 1.1rem;
		margin: 0.9rem 0 0.3rem;
		padding: 1rem;
		border-radius: 12px;
		background: var(--color-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	.octet-demo-group {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.4rem;
	}

	.octet-demo-label {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 1rem;
		color: #fff3d6;
		text-shadow: 0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb, #ffae2e 60%, transparent);
	}

	.octet-demo-tiles {
		display: flex;
		gap: 0.25rem;
	}

	/* --- IPv4 vs IPv6 comparison table — same conventions as the ASCII
	   quick-reference table on the How Computers Store Text page. */
	.compare-table-wrap {
		margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
		overflow-x: auto;
		border-radius: 10px;
		background: var(--color-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	.compare-table {
		width: 100%;
		border-collapse: collapse;
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}

	.compare-table th {
		padding: 0.5rem 0.9rem;
		text-align: left;
		font-family: var(--font-sans);
		font-size: 0.68rem;
		font-weight: 700;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.06em;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	.compare-table td {
		padding: 0.5rem 0.9rem;
		line-height: 1.4;
		border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-border) 45%, transparent);
	}

	.compare-table tbody tr:last-child td {
		border-bottom: none;
	}

	.compare-standard {
		font-weight: 700;
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	.compare-bits {
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.compare-addresses {
		color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 70%, var(--color-text-muted));
	}

	/* --- Scale bar: a bespoke two-row comparison, not a literal proportion
	   (rendering IPv4's true ~0.00000001% share would be an invisible
	   sliver) — the caption underneath says so explicitly. */
	.scale-bar {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.6rem;
		margin: 1rem auto 0;
		max-width: 26rem;
	}

	.scale-row {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 3rem 1fr 6.5rem;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.6rem;
	}

	.scale-label {
		text-align: right;
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.scale-track {
		height: 0.9rem;
		border-radius: 999px;
		background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-border) 60%, transparent);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
		overflow: hidden;
	}

	.scale-fill {
		height: 100%;
		border-radius: 999px;
	}

	.scale-fill--v4 {
		min-width: 4px;
		background: color-mix(in srgb, #ffae2e 55%, var(--color-text-muted));
	}

	.scale-fill--v6 {
		background: linear-gradient(90deg, #ffae2e, #fff3d6);
		box-shadow: 0 0 10px color-mix(in srgb, #ffae2e 55%, transparent);
	}

	.scale-value {
		text-align: left;
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	.scale-caption {
		margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		font-style: italic;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	/* --- Full IPv6 address, shown like a code block. */
	.ipv6-address-display {
		margin: 0.9rem auto 0.3rem;
		padding: 0.7rem 1rem;
		max-width: 28rem;
		border-radius: 10px;
		background: var(--color-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 0.95rem;
		letter-spacing: 0.03em;
		color: #fff3d6;
		text-shadow: 0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb, #ffae2e 50%, transparent);
		word-break: break-all;
	}

	/* --- Hex-to-binary tie-in visual: same grouped-tiles-with-label
	   language as the octet demo above, hex digit on top this time since
	   the reader is starting from the hex and looking underneath it. */
	.hexbit-demo {
		display: flex;
		align-items: flex-start;
		justify-content: center;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.9rem;
		margin: 0.9rem 0 0.3rem;
		padding: 1rem;
		border-radius: 12px;
		background: var(--color-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	.hexbit-demo-group {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.4rem;
	}

	.hexbit-demo-label {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		color: #fff3d6;
		text-shadow: 0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb, #ffae2e 60%, transparent);
	}

	.hexbit-demo-tiles {
		display: flex;
		gap: 0.25rem;
	}

	.learn-pagination {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}

	.learn-pagination-link {
		color: #ffae2e;
		font-weight: 600;
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	.learn-pagination-link:hover {
		text-decoration: underline;
	}

	.learn-pagination-next {
		margin-left: auto;
	}

	.learn-pagination-soon {
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		font-weight: 600;
		font-style: italic;
	}
</style>
